<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (Zara could choose to maintain status quo. To do this, it will continue to run its business mostly out of its distribution center in Arteixo, Spain, which sorts, packages, and ships new products to every store in the world twice a week. They have a quick-response system that relies on local workshops and factories in Spain and Portugal for fashion-forward items and some offshore sourcing for essentials would also continue to be the production model. This keeps Zara moving at its usual pace, releasing new designs every six weeks, making them much more frequently than most of its rivals.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 81 0 R
/E (To draw customers, the business would continue to spend very little on advertising \(about 0.3% of sales\) and rely on prominent store locations, striking window displays, and word-of-mouth marketing. With regular deliveries and small production batches, markdowns would be limited and customers would continue to return for new products. Zara would continue to absorb higher freight and import expenses for far-off markets and continue to charge higher prices overseas \(such as around 209% of Spain's pricing in the U.S. and 231% in Japan\) while accepting the current cost and price trade-offs concurrently. With the planned second distribution facility in Zaragoza, capacity might still be increased, but the primary logistics structure would stay in Spain.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
